        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Treikort',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Treikort is a unique Icelandic trick-taking game for exactly three players using a specially selected 27-card deck. The game features 'BÃ­sefarnir' (Sevens) that are unbeatable when led but worthless when following. A 'Pope' title is earned by winning 13 tricks across 3 consecutive games, granting card-swapping privileges.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Treikort was played widely in Iceland up to the last century. Old Danish games manuals describe a different game also called Treikort; the Icelandic form is distinct. It is sometimes called "3-man Alkort". The game is documented in Ólafur Davíðsson\'s writings on Icelandic amusements (1887–1903, Copenhagen) and in Spilabók AB by Þ. Guðmundsson (1989, Reykjavík).',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two or more players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '27 cards: Qâ™£ (highest non-7), 2â™ , Kâ™¦, 2â™¥, 4â™£, 8â™ , 9â™¥, 9â™¦, all A, J, 6, 8 (excl 8â™ ) + 4 Sevens (BÃ­sefarnir).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal 9 cards each (3 at a time).',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
